(1.3 Expressing Concentration of Solutions) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Several methods are used to express the concentration of a solution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Definition: Mass percentage of a component in a solution is the mass of that) Tj
T*
(component expressed as a percentage of the total mass of the solution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Mass percentage of A equals the mass of A divided by total mass of solution,) Tj
T*
(times one hundred.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Definition: Molarity \(M\) is the number of moles of solute dissolved per litre of) Tj
T*
(solution. It depends on temperature because volume changes with temperature.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Formula: M equals moles of solute divided by volume of solution in litres.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Definition: Molality \(m\) is the number of moles of solute per kilogram of) Tj
T*
(solvent. Molality is temperature-independent because mass does not change with) Tj
T*
(temperature.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Formula: m equals moles of solute divided by mass of solvent in kilograms.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Definition: Mole fraction of a component is the number of moles of that) Tj
T*
(component divided by the total number of moles of all components in the) Tj
T*
(solution.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Example 1.1: Calculate the molality of a solution containing 4.0 grams of NaOH) Tj
T*
(dissolved in 250 grams of water. Molar mass of NaOH is 40 g per mol.) Tj
T*
(Solution: moles of NaOH equals 4.0 divided by 40 equals 0.1 mol. Mass of solvent) Tj
T*
(in kg equals 0.250. Molality equals 0.1 divided by 0.250 equals 0.4 mol per kg.) Tj
ET

endstream
