(1.4 Solubility) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Definition: Solubility of a substance is the maximum amount that can be) Tj
T*
(dissolved in a specified amount of solvent at a specified temperature.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Solubility of a solid in a liquid depends on the nature of the solute and) Tj
T*
(solvent, on temperature, and weakly on pressure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Henry's Law: The partial pressure of the gas in vapour phase is proportional to) Tj
T*
(the mole fraction of the gas in the solution. Mathematically, p equals K H) Tj
T*
(times x, where K H is the Henry's law constant.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Example 1.2: If N2 gas is bubbled through water at 293 K and 1 atm, calculate) Tj
T*
(the mole fraction of N2 in water. K H for N2 at 293 K is 76.48 kbar.) Tj
T*
(Solution: x equals p divided by K H equals 1 atm divided by 76.48 kbar. Converting) Tj
T*
(1 atm to 0.987 bar, x equals 0.987 divided by 76480 equals 1.29 times 10 to the) Tj
T*
(minus 5.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(1.5 Vapour Pressure of Liquid Solutions) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Raoult's Law for a solution of two volatile components: the partial vapour) Tj
T*
(pressure of each component is directly proportional to its mole fraction in the) Tj
T*
(solution. p1 equals p1-naught times x1, and p2 equals p2-naught times x2, where) Tj
T*
(p1-naught and p2-naught are vapour pressures of pure components.) Tj
