T*
(number of solute particles, not on their nature.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The four colligative properties are: relative lowering of vapour pressure,) Tj
T*
(elevation of boiling point, depression of freezing point, and osmotic pressure.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Elevation of boiling point: delta T b equals K b times m, where K b is the) Tj
T*
(ebullioscopic constant and m is the molality of the solute.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Depression of freezing point: delta T f equals K f times m, where K f is the) Tj
T*
(cryoscopic constant.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Osmotic pressure: pi equals C R T, where C is the molar concentration of the) Tj
T*
(solute, R is the gas constant, and T is the temperature in Kelvin.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Example 1.3: 18 grams of glucose \(molar mass 180\) is dissolved in 1 kg of water.) Tj
T*
(Calculate the freezing point depression. K f for water is 1.86 K kg per mol.) Tj
T*
(Solution: molality m equals 18 divided by 180 divided by 1 equals 0.1 mol per) Tj
T*
(kg. Delta T f equals 1.86 times 0.1 equals 0.186 K. So the freezing point of) Tj
T*
(the solution is minus 0.186 degrees Celsius.) Tj
T*
() Tj
